Stepbirds SoundCloud Uploader: session prompt (v2)

    Paste everything below into Claude Code on the practice laptop. Before you start: have ffmpeg available (or let Claude install it with winget), know where the old script and the Excel run log live if you can, and have SoundCloud API app credentials ready (see "Credentials").

Who we are

We're The Stepbirds, a rock band with a rotating crew. For about six years we've recorded nearly every session (mostly Wednesday nights and weekends) in our practice space, Room I at the Brickyard building. Note: the session folders on disk say "Brickhouse Room I", not "Brickyard". Use the folder names as they are.

Every session Studio One creates a folder in E:\Studio One\Songs. We record the room with two mics, so most takes are a mono pair: Media\Track 1.wav + Media\Track 2.wav, and later takes in the same session are Track 1(2).wav + Track 2(2).wav, Track 1(3).wav + Track 2(3).wav, and so on.

A snapshot of the folder tree (from about September 2026) is dir_songs.txt in https://github.com/jeffress/stepbirds. It is about 1 MB. Do not read it whole. Use Select-String, a short script, or a fresh Get-ChildItem on the live drive to summarize it.

The goal

Get every recording we want online onto our SoundCloud account, keep a clear record of what's up and what isn't, then keep SoundCloud in sync automatically as new sessions land. About 491 tracks are already uploaded from an earlier effort.

"Approved" means: every take that is not on the exclusion list (below) and passes the quality checks.

Hard rules (never break these)

    E:\Studio One\Songs is read-only. It holds our only master copies. Never create, rename, move, edit or delete anything under it, including .song files, History, Cache, or Studio One's own files. Open files read-only. Do all work in a staging folder on a drive with enough free space (a stereo bounce can be ~2.7 GB; check free space before each bounce and delete staged files after a confirmed upload. Find previous efforts' temp folder and use that if possible).
    Never ask me to paste passwords, tokens or secrets into this chat, and never write them into the repo, the logs, or any file Claude reads back. See "Credentials".
    Nothing irreversible on SoundCloud without my yes. Deleting a track, removing tracks from a playlist, or overwriting a playlist's track list requires a dry-run report first and my explicit confirmation in this chat.
    Phase 1 is read-only everywhere. No uploads and no SoundCloud changes until I've approved the inventory report.

Credentials

The SoundCloud API uses OAuth (client ID + client secret, then an access token and refresh token). The script needs those, not our account password.

    The first attempt was built with ChatGPT under a corporate account that has since been shut off, so that conversation history is gone. The SoundCloud side may still be fine: first check whether the old script or its config references an existing SoundCloud API app (client ID) we can keep using. Only if not, walk me through registering a new app for the band's account; I'll do the registration and the browser login myself. SoundCloud has at times limited new app registrations, so check the current process first.
    Store secrets with PowerShell SecretManagement / Windows Credential Manager (preferred), or in a file outside the working folder's git checkout and outside E:\Studio One\Songs, readable only by my Windows user. Add any local config files to .gitignore.
    Handle token refresh in the script so it can run unattended.

