Improve the old script rather than rewriting it, unless it's unsalvageable. If so, say why in one paragraph before replacing it.

Phase 1: Inventory and dry run (read-only)

Keep using the existing Excel run log. Copy the original workbook into a working folder on this laptop (for example D:\Stepbirds-uploader\, never under E:\Studio One\Songs), keep the original untouched as a backup, and continue in the copy. Keep its existing columns and rows and add columns as needed. All logs, the manifest, exclusions.txt and backups live on this laptop, not in GitHub. Back up the workbook (a timestamped copy) before every run that writes to it. If the workbook is open in Excel and locked, wait and retry, and never write a half-finished file.

Use that workbook as the single source of truth (manifest), for example one sheet per take and one sheet for the run log, unless you find a good reason to keep the manifest separate; tell me if so. One row per take, with at least: session folder, take number, source files, sizes, duration, recorded date, year/playlist, intended SoundCloud title, status (pending / uploaded / excluded / skipped / error), SoundCloud track ID and URL, playlist, last error, last updated.

To fill it:

    Local side: walk every session folder's Media subfolder. Pair takes by their (n) suffix.
    SoundCloud side: list all our tracks and all six yearly playlists (2021 to 2026) via the API.
    Match existing uploads to local takes using the old run log first, then title, then duration (±2 s). Record the SoundCloud track ID for each match. List anything that doesn't match on either side.
    Report: total takes, already uploaded, still pending, excluded, skipped (with reasons), uploaded but missing from a yearly playlist (or in the wrong one), and SoundCloud tracks with no local match.

Edge cases already visible in dir_songs.txt. Handle each and report counts:

    Tiny takes: about 81 Track 1 files are under 10 MB (false starts or soundchecks, often ~800 KB). Propose a minimum duration (e.g. 60 s) and list what it would skip. Don't upload these by default.
    Unpaired takes: a Track 1(n) with no Track 2(n) or vice versa (about 6, e.g. 2022-11-30 takes 8 to 10, 2025-09-26 takes 3 and 4). Upload as mono duplicated to both channels, or flag. Ask me which.
    Name variants: Track  1.wav (two spaces) exists alongside Track 1.wav. Match names flexibly.
    More than two tracks per take (Track 3.wav and up, e.g. 2021-09-06 has Tracks 1 to 6) and multitrack sessions with named inputs (Snare, Kick Drum, Overheads, Vocal 1, Guitar 1 and so on, about 12 sessions, e.g. "2026-04-22 Brickhouse First Try At Multitracking"). A two-mic bounce doesn't apply here. Flag them for me. If a finished mixdown exists (Mixdown\ or Bounces\ folders, e.g. 2023-12-06, 2026-02-15 (2), 2026-05-27), propose uploading that instead.
    Overdubs vs. new takes: when one folder has several pairs, parse the session's top-level .song file to tell sequential takes (new recording) from overdubs (layered over an earlier take at the same timeline position). A .song file is, I believe, a ZIP of XML files. Verify that, and research the format. Ignore .song copies under History\ and .autosave files.
    Folders with no Media (e.g. 2021-10-12 Brickhouse Room I(10)) should be skipped and logged.
    Undated or titled folders: "Maniac On The Vapor Trail" and "Valley Of Despair" have no date in the name. Get the year from file timestamps for the playlist, and tell me. Folders like 2026-04-26 Prayer carry a real song title; use it in the SoundCloud title.
    Notes in folder names, e.g. (4 big blank space in middle) and (2) james and walker bass lead then mask. Keep the text in the title or description, and flag the "blank space" one for me.
    Other bands' recordings won't be obvious from names. Don't guess. That's what the exclusion list is for.

Naming and audio

    Bounce each pair into one stereo file (Track 1 = left, Track 2 = right) with ffmpeg, keeping the source sample rate and bit depth. Recommend whether to upload WAV or FLAC (FLAC is lossless and about half the size, which makes uploads faster). Respect SoundCloud's per-file size and length limits, and check the current limits.
    Use the title convention the existing 491 uploads already follow (work it out from the old script and the API listing, and show me). If there isn't one, propose: YYYY-MM-DD Brickhouse Room I - Take N (or the song title when the folder has one).
    Ask me once whether new uploads should be public or private. Private is safer until we've listened through.

Playlists

    Add each upload to its year's playlist (by recording date). A new year needs a new playlist; create it using the same naming as the existing six.
    Updating a playlist via the API replaces its whole track list, so always read it first, add to it, write it back, and keep a local backup of every playlist's track list before changing it.

Exclusion list

    A plain text file, exclusions.txt, that we edit by hand. One entry per line: a session folder, or a folder plus take number. # starts a comment, and we'll often add a reason ("not us", "bad take").
    The script never uploads anything that's on it.
    If something on the list is already on SoundCloud, set it to private first (reversible), and log it. Actual deletion only happens after I confirm a dry-run list of exactly what will be deleted. Record the outcome in the manifest.

Being a good SoundCloud API citizen

    Look up SoundCloud's current API rate limits and our account's upload quota, and stay well under them: one upload at a time, a pause between uploads, and a daily cap (start with something like 25 uploads a day and tell me the reasoning).
    On HTTP 429 or 5xx: back off exponentially, honour Retry-After, and stop for the day after repeated failures.
    Make every step resumable and idempotent: after a crash, the manifest says exactly what's done, and nothing is ever uploaded twice.

Running by itself (and saving AI tokens)

    The uploader and the watcher must be plain PowerShell run by Windows Task Scheduler, with no AI involved. Claude is only for building and fixing it. Don't keep a Claude session running just to monitor uploads.
    Watcher: on each run (e.g. hourly, and only when the laptop is idle), look for new session folders or takes. Only treat a take as ready once its files haven't changed for at least 30 minutes, so we never grab a recording that's still in progress or a session that's still open in Studio One.
    Logs: keep appending to the existing run log so the handoff stays readable. Use one line per action with a timestamp, in plain English. Also write a short daily summary ("uploaded 12, 3 errors, 140 pending".)
    Errors go to a separate problems report with what failed, why, and the suggested fix, so I can read it without asking Claude.
    Add a -WhatIf / -DryRun switch, and a -Status command that prints the summary from the manifest.

How to work with me in this session

    Work in phases (0, 1, then build, then a test upload of 2 or 3 takes, then the bulk run). Stop for my OK after Phase 0, Phase 1 and the test upload.
    Be thrifty with tokens: summarize big files with commands instead of reading them, and don't re-read files you've already understood.
    Keep HANDOFF.md up to date in the working folder on this laptop as you go: what's done, what's next, open questions, and where everything lives. If this session runs out of usage, a fresh session should be able to continue from that file. Stop at a clean checkpoint rather than mid-change.
    The log, manifest, exclusions and handoff notes stay on this laptop. If you put the script code in https://github.com/jeffress/stepbirds, use a branch, and never commit secrets, the Excel log, or audio.
    When unsure, ask one clear question rather than guessing.
